### What is a fixed-window counter?

The fixed-window counter divides time into equal windows, say one minute, and keeps a per-client counter that resets at each boundary. Each request increments the counter, and once it reaches the limit, further requests are rejected. It is trivial to implement and cheap in memory. Its well-known flaw is the boundary burst: a client can send a full limit at the end of one window and another full limit at the start of the next, producing up to 2x the intended rate.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class FixedWindowRateLimiter {

    private final int maxRequests;
    private final long windowSizeMs;
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, WindowCounter> counters
        = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public FixedWindowRateLimiter(int maxRequests, long windowSizeMs) {
        this.maxRequests = maxRequests;
        this.windowSizeMs = windowSizeMs;
    }

    public boolean allowRequest(String clientId) {
        long currentWindow = System.currentTimeMillis() / windowSizeMs;
        WindowCounter counter = counters.compute(clientId, (k, v) -> {
            if (v == null || v.window != currentWindow) {
                return new WindowCounter(currentWindow);
            }
            return v;
        });
        return counter.count.incrementAndGet() <= maxRequests;
    }

    private static class WindowCounter {
        final long window;
        final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
        WindowCounter(long window) { this.window = window; }
    }
}
```

```text
Boundary burst problem (limit = 100/min):
  10:59:55 -> 100 requests (allowed, fills the window)
  11:00:00 -> window resets
  11:00:05 -> 100 requests (allowed, new window)
  Result: 200 requests in 10 seconds!
```

### What is a sliding-window log?

The sliding-window log stores a timestamp for every request and counts how many fall within the trailing window. This is perfectly accurate but memory-expensive, since you store one entry per request.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentLinkedDeque;

public class SlidingWindowLogLimiter {

    private final int maxRequests;
    private final long windowMs;
    private final ConcurrentLinkedDeque<Long> timestamps
        = new ConcurrentLinkedDeque<>();

    public SlidingWindowLogLimiter(int maxRequests, long windowMs) {
        this.maxRequests = maxRequests;
        this.windowMs = windowMs;
    }

    public synchronized boolean allowRequest() {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
        long cutoff = now - windowMs;

        // Remove expired timestamps
        while (!timestamps.isEmpty() && timestamps.peekFirst() < cutoff) {
            timestamps.pollFirst();
        }

        if (timestamps.size() < maxRequests) {
            timestamps.addLast(now);
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

```text
Memory cost at 10,000 requests/sec with 60s window:
  Entries stored: up to 600,000 timestamps
  Memory: ~4.8 MB per client key (8 bytes per Long)
  Accurate: yes, no boundary burst
```

### What is a sliding-window counter?

The sliding-window counter keeps just two counters, the current window and the previous window, and estimates the rate by weighting the previous window's count by how much of it still overlaps the trailing window. This smooths the boundary burst to a small approximation error while using almost no memory.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

public class SlidingWindowCounterLimiter {

    private final int maxRequests;
    private final long windowMs;
    private int previousCount;
    private int currentCount;
    private long currentWindowStart;

    public SlidingWindowCounterLimiter(int maxRequests, long windowMs) {
        this.maxRequests = maxRequests;
        this.windowMs = windowMs;
        this.currentWindowStart = System.currentTimeMillis();
    }

    public synchronized boolean allowRequest() {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
        long elapsed = now - currentWindowStart;

        if (elapsed >= windowMs) {
            previousCount = currentCount;
            currentCount = 0;
            currentWindowStart = now;
            elapsed = 0;
        }

        // Weight previous window by overlap
        double overlapRatio = 1.0 - ((double) elapsed / windowMs);
        double estimatedCount =
            (previousCount * overlapRatio) + currentCount;

        if (estimatedCount < maxRequests) {
            currentCount++;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

```text
Memory: 2 integers per client key (trivial)
Accuracy: small approximation error (~1-2%)
Boundary burst: eliminated by weighted blending
```

### What is a token bucket?

The token bucket refills tokens at a steady rate up to a capacity. Each request removes one token. Because the bucket can hold accumulated tokens, it permits a controlled burst up to that capacity when the client has been idle, then settles to the refill rate.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

public class TokenBucketLimiter {

    private final int capacity;
    private final double refillRate; // tokens per millisecond
    private double tokens;
    private long lastRefillTime;

    public TokenBucketLimiter(int capacity, int refillPerSecond) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.refillRate = refillPerSecond / 1000.0;
        this.tokens = capacity;
        this.lastRefillTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
    }

    public synchronized boolean allowRequest() {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
        double elapsed = now - lastRefillTime;
        tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + (elapsed * refillRate));
        lastRefillTime = now;

        if (tokens >= 1.0) {
            tokens -= 1.0;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

```text
Token bucket (capacity=20, refill=10/sec):
  Client idle for 2 seconds -> 20 tokens available
  Burst of 20 requests -> all allowed (controlled burst)
  Next request -> must wait 100ms for 1 token to refill
  Steady state: 10 requests/sec average
```

### What is a leaky bucket?

The leaky bucket drains requests at a constant rate, smoothing bursty input into steady output. It offers no burst allowance and may add queuing delay, but it is ideal when a downstream system can only handle a steady rate.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;

public class LeakyBucketLimiter {

    private final LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable> queue;
    private final int drainRatePerSecond;

    public LeakyBucketLimiter(int queueCapacity, int drainRatePerSecond) {
        this.queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(queueCapacity);
        this.drainRatePerSecond = drainRatePerSecond;
        startDraining();
    }

    public boolean submit(Runnable task) {
        return queue.offer(task); // false if queue full
    }

    private void startDraining() {
        Thread drainer = new Thread(() -> {
            long intervalMs = 1000 / drainRatePerSecond;
            while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
                try {
                    Runnable task = queue.take();
                    task.run();
                    Thread.sleep(intervalMs);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            }
        });
        drainer.setDaemon(true);
        drainer.start();
    }
}
```

```text
Leaky bucket (queue=50, drain=10/sec):
  Burst of 50 requests -> all queued
  Processing: 1 every 100ms (strictly constant)
  51st request -> rejected (queue full)
  Total drain time for 50 requests: 5 seconds
```

### How do the algorithms compare?

| Algorithm | Simplicity | Memory | Boundary Burst | Burst Tolerance | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Fixed window | Trivial | Tiny | Yes (2x) | No | Simple internal limits |
| Sliding log | Medium | High | No | No | Exact precision needed |
| Sliding counter | Medium | Tiny | No (~1%) | No | Production rate limits |
| Token bucket | Medium | Tiny | No | Yes (controlled) | Public API limits |
| Leaky bucket | Medium | Queue size | No | No (queued) | Protecting downstream |

### How do you enforce limits across multiple instances?

If each instance keeps its own counter, N instances each allow the full limit and the effective limit is N times too high. The state must live in Redis with atomic operations via Lua scripts.

```lua
-- Redis Lua script for distributed token bucket
local key = KEYS[1]
local capacity = tonumber(ARGV[1])
local refill_rate = tonumber(ARGV[2])  -- tokens per second
local now = tonumber(ARGV[3])          -- current timestamp ms

local data = redis.call('HMGET', key, 'tokens', 'last_refill')
local tokens = tonumber(data[1]) or capacity
local last_refill = tonumber(data[2]) or now

-- Refill tokens
local elapsed = (now - last_refill) / 1000.0
tokens = math.min(capacity, tokens + (elapsed * refill_rate))

local allowed = 0
if tokens >= 1 then
    tokens = tokens - 1
    allowed = 1
end

redis.call('HMSET', key, 'tokens', tokens, 'last_refill', now)
redis.call('EXPIRE', key, math.ceil(capacity / refill_rate) * 2)

return allowed
```

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.data.redis.core.script.DefaultRedisScript;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.util.Collections;

@Component
public class DistributedTokenBucket {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final DefaultRedisScript<Long> script;

    public DistributedTokenBucket(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
        this.script = new DefaultRedisScript<>();
        this.script.setScriptText("-- Lua script above");
        this.script.setResultType(Long.class);
    }

    public boolean allowRequest(String clientId,
                                 int capacity,
                                 int refillPerSecond) {
        Long result = redis.execute(script,
            Collections.singletonList("ratelimit:" + clientId),
            String.valueOf(capacity),
            String.valueOf(refillPerSecond),
            String.valueOf(System.currentTimeMillis()));
        return result != null && result == 1;
    }
}
```

```text
Without shared counter (3 instances, limit 100/min):
  Instance 1: allows 100
  Instance 2: allows 100
  Instance 3: allows 100
  Effective limit: 300/min (3x too high!)

With Redis shared counter (3 instances, limit 100/min):
  All instances check Redis atomically
  Effective limit: exactly 100/min (correct)
```

### What are common mistakes with rate limiting?

The most common mistake is keeping the counter in local memory, which makes the limit per-instance instead of global. Another is not returning a `Retry-After` header with a 429 response, leaving clients guessing when to retry.

```java
package com.bank.ratelimit;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class RateLimitedController {

    private final DistributedTokenBucket limiter;

    public RateLimitedController(DistributedTokenBucket limiter) {
        this.limiter = limiter;
    }

    @GetMapping("/api/accounts/{id}/balance")
    public ResponseEntity<?> getBalance(String clientId, String id) {
        if (!limiter.allowRequest(clientId, 100, 10)) {
            // CORRECT: return 429 with Retry-After
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS)
                .header("Retry-After", "5")
                .header("X-RateLimit-Remaining", "0")
                .body("Rate limit exceeded. Try again in 5 seconds.");
        }
        return ResponseEntity.ok("Balance: 150000.00");
    }
}
```
